  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SCALING AN INNOVATIVE COMMUNITY-RUN ECCE PROGRAMME IN GHANA.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">worldwide, more than 250 million young childrenthe majority of whom are in low- and middle-income countriesare at risk of not achieving their developmental potential due to poverty, poor health and nutrition, and deficient care and stimulation. to improve early childhood care and education (ecce) and school readiness, education partners are supporting the government of ghana to run parent-led, low-cost, highly effective educational playgroups for children ages four to six. a randomized controlled trial (rct) on education partners model found that the ecce program significantly improves child development, including cognitive, socio-emotional, and health, which are critical components of school readiness. as the government starts to implement the model, education partners are training education officials and teachers to prepare and empower mothers to run playgroups at their childrens kindergartens. with support from development innovation ventures, education partners will work with the government of ghana to scale the program from six to 36 districts and catalyze funding to reach 60 districtsincluding 1.2 million childrenacross the northern region by the end of 2028.</narrative>
  </description>
